<Base title="Vector search in PostgreSQL" description="Derive exact neighbours, observe pgvector's filtered approximate search, and distinguish distance recall from useful semantic results.">
<article class="visual-essay relational-design">
 <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">PostgreSQL</p><h1>Vector search in PostgreSQL</h1><TitleLine type="PostgreSQL deep dive" version="PostgreSQL 18 · pgvector 0.8.7"><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/full-text-search/">Full-text search ↗</a></TitleLine></header>
<ArticleBody>
 <p>A vector is a list of numbers. Vector search compares those lists with a distance function and retrieves nearby ones. An embedding model can turn text, images or other inputs into vectors whose proximity is useful for a task—for example, finding documentation related to a question that uses different words.</p>
 <p>That creates two separate questions. Does the model place useful answers nearby? And does the database retrieve those nearby vectors? An exact nearest-neighbour query can answer the second perfectly for its stored vectors while the first still produces disappointing search results. An approximate index introduces a further tradeoff: it may avoid comparing every vector by accepting that some nearer results will be missed.</p>
 <p><a href="https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector">pgvector</a> adds vector types, distance operators and approximate indexes to PostgreSQL as an extension. They work beside ordinary rows and filters. We will start with distances small enough to calculate, then inspect a real filtered HNSW experiment. The measurements concern retrieval from synthetic vectors, not the quality of an embedding model.</p>

 <section id="exact"><h2>Exact neighbours</h2>
 <p>Our first table has four three-dimensional vectors and a tenant ID. The query vector is <code>[1,0,0]</code>. All third coordinates are zero, which lets us draw the example on a flat plane without discarding a dimension that affects the answer.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Four vectors and their distance to the query"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Tenant</th><th>Vector</th><th>Euclidean distance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>7</td><td>[1, 0, 0]</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>8</td><td>[0.9, 0.1, 0]</td><td>√0.02 ≈ 0.1414</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>7</td><td>[0.8, 0.2, 0]</td><td>√0.08 ≈ 0.2828</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>7</td><td>[0, 1, 0]</td><td>√2 ≈ 1.4142</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p>Euclidean distance takes the difference on each coordinate, squares those differences, adds them and takes the square root. For point 3, that is √((1−0.8)² + (0−0.2)² + 0²), or √0.08. Point 1 has distance zero because it is identical to the query.</p>
 <Points />
 <p>With pgvector's Euclidean distance operator <code>&lt;-&gt;</code>, the closest two rows belonging to tenant 7 are:</p>
 <Sql code={exact} label="An exact filtered nearest-neighbour query" />
 <p>The result is IDs 1 and 3. Filtering the eligible rows before the top-two choice is part of the question. If we choose the closest two across all tenants and then remove tenant 8, the candidate list contains only ID 1. There is another eligible neighbour, but the limited list has already discarded it.</p>
 <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-vector-filter">distance and filtering lesson</a> reproduces this small calculation with ordinary numeric SQL columns. It is explicitly a model of distance and candidate filtering. The downloadable server experiment below runs actual pgvector operators and HNSW; the browser model does not claim to implement that index.</p>
 <p>A distance function defines “near.” Cosine distance compares direction; Euclidean distance also responds to magnitude. Use the metric expected by the embedding model, and keep its dimensions and model version consistent. Comparing vectors from unrelated models simply because they have the same length does not give their coordinates a shared meaning.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="approximate"><h2>Approximate search with HNSW</h2>
 <p>Without an approximate index, pgvector calculates an exact nearest-neighbour answer. A selective relational filter can make that a reasonable design: a tenant might have only a few hundred eligible vectors, even when the whole table is large. An ordinary index on tenant or another filter can help find that smaller set before calculating distances.</p>
 <p>HNSW takes a different route. Its upper graph layers contain progressively smaller subsets of the vectors. Starting at an entry point in an upper layer, search compares neighbouring points with the query and moves towards a nearer one. When that local route stops improving, it descends to a denser layer using the point it found as the next starting point.</p>
 <p>At the bottom layer, search explores neighbours while maintaining a bounded set of promising results. Finding a closer point can admit it and displace a farther candidate. This makes it possible to inspect a small region instead of calculating the whole table's ordering. But a point reached only through an unexplored connection can remain unseen even if it is nearer than a retained result. Widening exploration changes the chance of finding it. The <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09320">original HNSW paper</a> develops this layered search and its candidate-selection rules.</p>
 <Sql code={hnsw} label="An HNSW index for Euclidean distance" />
 <p><code>vector_l2_ops</code> connects this index to Euclidean comparisons. An index built for one metric is not automatically the right access path for another. The indexed nearest-neighbour form also matters: request the distance operator in ascending <code>ORDER BY</code> with a limit, then inspect the actual plan. Rewriting it into an arbitrary score expression can prevent the expected index use.</p>
 <p>The search setting <code>hnsw.ef_search</code> controls its dynamic candidate list. More candidates can improve recall while increasing work. Tenant and publication filters are still SQL conditions; with an approximate scan they may remove candidates after the index finds them. A globally nearby region dominated by other tenants can therefore leave few eligible results. The four-point example showed the shape of this problem even though its candidate selection was exact.</p>
 <p>pgvector also offers IVFFlat, which groups vectors into lists and searches selected lists. Its training and probing choices differ from HNSW's graph and memory costs. Choosing between them needs the actual data distribution, update workload and recall requirements; the presence of two index types does not establish which will fit.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="experiment"><h2>Tenant filters and iterative HNSW scans</h2>
 <p><a href={experiment} download="vector-experiment.sql">Download the executed PostgreSQL/pgvector experiment</a>. Run it with <code>psql -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -d your_lab -f vector-experiment.sql</code> against a disposable database whose server has pgvector installed. The script enables the extension in that database and replaces only its named practice tables. It records the engine and extension versions. This run used PostgreSQL 18.6 and pgvector 0.8.7.</p>
 <p>The larger table has 10,000 rows with sixteen coordinates. Coordinate <code>d</code> for row <code>n</code> is generated as <code>sin(n × d × 0.73)</code>, converted to the vector's single-precision representation. Tenant is <code>n % 50</code>, giving tenant 7 two hundred rows. We use row 123's vector as the fixed query. That row belongs to tenant 23, so its own zero-distance match is ineligible.</p>
 <p>Before building HNSW, the script calculates and stores the closest ten tenant-7 IDs by exact distance. That is the reference answer. After building the index, it runs the same filtered question with different settings and compares returned identities to those ten. The query vector, eligibility filter and source data stay fixed.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed filtered vector retrieval results"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Observed plan</th><th>Returned</th><th>Exact top-ten matches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ef_search 40, iteration off</td><td>HNSW index scan</td><td>0</td><td>0 / 10</td></tr><tr><td>ef_search 200, iteration off</td><td>Sequential scan and top-N sort</td><td>10</td><td>10 / 10</td></tr><tr><td>ef_search 40, strict iteration</td><td>HNSW index scan</td><td>10</td><td>9 / 10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p>The first plan removed forty candidates by the tenant filter and returned none. There were two hundred eligible rows in the table, but none of these candidates belonged to tenant 7. A success response with an empty result did not mean “the tenant has no documents.” It described what this approximate scan found under its search budget.</p>
 <p>The second result needs its plan to be understood. Increasing <code>ef_search</code> also changed the planner's cost comparison. PostgreSQL chose an exact sequential scan over the table, filtered two hundred eligible vectors and sorted for the closest ten. Its perfect agreement is not evidence that HNSW with this setting achieved perfect recall. Measuring only the result or elapsed time would have hidden the method change.</p>
 <p>The third run enabled iterative scanning. It continued searching when filtering left too few candidates, removed 576 ineligible rows in the recorded run, and returned ten eligible rows. Nine belonged to the exact top ten. The setting changed the stopping behaviour, not the definition of eligibility.</p>
 <Sql code={query} label="Scope iterative-search settings to one transaction and inspect the plan" />
 <p>The <code>BEGIN</code>/<code>COMMIT</code> pair keeps these <code>SET LOCAL</code> choices on this connection's current transaction. A real handler would set the policy, execute its parameterised query, consume the response and end the transaction; it should not accidentally leave a pooled connection with another request's search policy.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="recall"><h2>Measuring recall against an exact result</h2>
 <p>Here <strong>recall at ten</strong> is the number of reference top-ten identities retrieved, divided by ten. The last run has recall 0.9. Returning ten rows is not enough to establish recall 1.0: the tenth row can be a slightly farther neighbour replacing one the graph search missed.</p>
 <p><code>strict_order</code> means the returned results follow their exact distance order. It does not mean that the returned set is the exact nearest set. Iterative scanning also has work and memory limits; it can stop before finding enough eligible rows. The <a href="https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector#iterative-index-scans">extension's iterative-scan documentation</a> describes those limits and the relaxed-order alternative.</p>
 <p>The graph construction and resulting candidates can vary between builds. An earlier build of this same fixture retrieved all ten with strict iteration; the preserved run retrieved nine. Both are observations, and neither establishes a general quality bound. Repeat the comparison over a representative query set, include restrictive filters and measure the tail of the misses. If distance ties occur at the boundary, define how equivalent answers count before reporting recall.</p>
 <p>These synthetic coordinates make retrieval behaviour reproducible enough to inspect, but do not represent document meaning. Semantic quality needs a separate set of questions and judgments about useful answers. A perfect exact match against a poor embedding is still poor search. Conversely, an approximate miss may or may not change usefulness; the application must evaluate that consequence rather than treating a recall percentage as a complete product score.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="application"><h2>Keeping embeddings current</h2>
 <p>An embedding is usually produced outside the database by a model. Suppose document revision 5 is sent to an embedding worker, then the document changes to revision 6 before the result returns. Storing revision 5's vector beside revision 6's text can make search retrieve a document for content it no longer contains.</p>
 <p>The handler needs a source revision and model identity alongside the vector. One application pattern accepts the returned vector only if the source is still the revision that was embedded:</p>
 <Sql code={embeddingUpdate} label="Application statement shape: reject a late result for an older revision" />
 <p>Here the worker binds the vector, source revision, model name and document ID to the parameters. A returned ID means the revision still matched when PostgreSQL performed the update. No returned row means this result was stale or its source was gone; the worker discards it or schedules the current revision. This is a statement pattern for the application's own schema, not another table created by the download.</p>
 <p>The search policy must also decide what to do while the newest revision lacks an embedding. It can omit that row, permit an explicitly stale representation, or use another retrieval path. The conditional write prevents late work from silently claiming freshness; it does not eliminate the interval needed to compute a new vector. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/adding-another-system/">derived-copy article</a> develops delivery, replay and withdrawal rules for that interval.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="choice"><h2>Choosing the search design</h2>
 <p>Begin with the exact filtered answer and the application's quality expectations. If the eligible set is small, exact distance calculations may be simpler and competitive. If it is large, compare approximate settings against that reference while tracking actual plans, latency, memory, index size and maintenance. A shared index across tenants creates a different candidate-search problem from an index over one tenant's data.</p>
 <p>Partial indexes or partitioned placement can narrow that problem for suitable workloads, with additional schema and operating costs. A tenant with ten vectors and one with ten million deserve separate measurements. Logical access control must remain in force regardless of where candidates come from; recall tuning cannot become permission to return another tenant's records.</p>
 <p>Lexical search is often a useful companion. Exact names and identifiers can matter even when an embedding regards a different document as nearby. Combine candidate sets with a defined method, such as ranks or a later relevance model. Raw cosine distance and a lexical score are not automatically on a meaningful common scale.</p>
 <p>Keeping the vectors in PostgreSQL can make joins, eligibility and row changes easier to coordinate. It also places vector storage, index builds and search work beside other database workloads. The case for a separate service should follow measured capacity or retrieval requirements and the cost of keeping its copy current—not the assumption that relational databases only understand scalar equality.</p>
 </section>
